licence. Each changed file carries a "Modified by the QUICraft project" note.)

QUICraft patch for Netty netty-4.2.19.Final (commit 64cc10f38ea5f5bd7eae48507817c66680d0afdc),
on top of 0001-0003: the TLS exporter that docs/protocol.md section 12.2 and 12.7 need.

Netty exposes no TLS exporter, so an application cannot derive a value that both ends of a QUIC
connection compute and a third party without the TLS secrets cannot. This patch binds BoringSSL's
SSL_export_keying_material, which is the RFC 8446 section 7.5 normal exporter (RFC 5705 before
TLS 1.3):

- JNI: BoringSSL.SSL_export_keying_material(ssl, label, context, length) calls
  SSL_export_keying_material with use_context = 1, so an empty context is a zero-length context
  and never an absent one, and with the label as the US-ASCII bytes the caller passes. It returns
  the exported bytes, or null when BoringSSL fails, which is also what happens before the
  handshake has completed. The native buffer is wiped before it is freed. The export is never the
  early exporter: RFC 8446 supplies the context explicitly on the normal one.
- BoringSSL.SSL_export_keying_material_supported() is a capability probe that exists only so Quic
  can ask whether the binding is there: the export itself needs an established TLS session to
  answer at all, so it cannot be probed directly. On upstream Netty's native (the other four
  platforms, section 11) both methods stay unbound and calling them throws UnsatisfiedLinkError.
- Quic.isKeyingMaterialExportSupported(): false when the classes are paired with a native without
  the binding, so an unpatched native degrades to a false capability probe instead of a crash.
- QuicChannel.exportKeyingMaterial(String label, byte[] context, int length): the accessor. A
  default method that throws UnsupportedOperationException, so no other QuicChannel implementation
  (or module) has to change. QuicheQuicChannel overrides it: it requires the channel's own event
  loop (anything else throws IllegalStateException) and returns null before the handshake has
  completed, when the channel is closed, or when the native export fails, so no caller can mistake
  a missing value for an empty one. The export runs under the connection's monitor
  (QuicheQuicConnection.exportKeyingMaterial), which is the SSL object that quiche owns and frees
  with the connection, in free() and in the finalizer.

Nothing else changes: the option is reached only by code that calls the new accessor, and the
native jar gains no dependency.

Applied by natives/build-netty-quic.sh with `git apply`, which ignores this text.

